<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cacicazgo of Maguana</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Taíno chiefdom of central Hispaniola whose cacique Caonabo destroyed the first Spanish settlement, La Navidad, in 1493.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Maguana occupied the mountainous centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, including the San Juan valley and the gold-bearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cibao">Cibao</a> uplands. It was one of five principal chiefdoms on the island when the Spanish arrived in 1492, with roots in earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> society.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its paramount cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a>, said by some sources to have come from the Lucayan islands, was a renowned war leader. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacaona">Anacaona</a> was the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohechio">Bohechío</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cacicazgo-of-jaragua">Jaragua</a>, linking the two chiefdoms. Its people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, held ballgames in plazas and worshipped zemís.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> destroyed the Spanish fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> in 1493. In 1495 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alonso-de-ojeda">Alonso de Ojeda</a> captured him by a ruse, and he died at sea on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Spanish forces then broke Maguana's resistance and imposed tribute in gold.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1200 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:5.87%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Caonabo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Taíno religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Taíno</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maguana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=cacicazgo-of-maguana" aria-label="Cacicazgo of Maguana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2770 1462 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cacicazgo of Maguana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3090 1718h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3090 1718h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Dominican Republic. <a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=cacicazgo-of-maguana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
